WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 4.3: Delegation, Service Accounts and Joining. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 4.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.250
3, Delegation, Service Accounts and
Joining.

3
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Your domain sa1.

4
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.150
lab exists, but only DC01 belongs to it.

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.800
You will see why a computer must use the
domain controller for DNS before it can

6
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:21.650
join, and you will join SRV01 and CLIENT01
in the GUI and with PowerShell.

7
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.500
You will put computer objects in the right
OU.

8
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:32.150
Then you will hand one small task to the
help desk, and read the permission it

9
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.600
creates.

10
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:39.450
Last, you will choose a service account
and build a group managed service account

11
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:40.900
for SRV01.

12
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.950
Joining starts with a question: where is a
domain controller for sa1.

13
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.400
lab?

14
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.850
The computer asks its DNS server for a
special SRV record, underscore ldap,

15
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.100
underscore tcp, dc, underscore msdcs.

16
00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:00.150
DC01 holds that record in its Active
Directory zones and answers: dc01.

17
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.600
sa1.

18
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.050
lab, port 389.

19
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:09.700
If DNS is blank, or still points at SRV01
from Learning Plan 3, nobody knows sa1.

20
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:15.550
lab, and you get the error that a domain
controller could not be contacted.

21
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.600
So the rule for every domain member is
simple: DNS server 192.

22
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.050
168.

23
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.500
50.

24
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.950
10.

25
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.200
Never a public DNS server.

26
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.650
The classic way to join is System
Properties.

27
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.100
Type sysdm.

28
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:37.950
cpl, or on Windows 11 go to Settings,
System, About, and select Domain or

29
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.400
workgroup.

30
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.450
On the Computer Name tab, select Change.

31
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:48.300
In the Computer Name and Domain Changes
dialog, under Member of, select Domain and

32
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:49.750
type sa1.

33
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.200
lab.

34
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.650
Select OK.

35
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.900
Windows asks for an account that may join
computers; in the lab that is SA1

36
00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.350
backslash Administrator.

37
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.200
You get a welcome message, and then a
restart.

38
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.650
Until that restart, the join is not
finished.

39
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.500
Here is the same job in PowerShell on
SRV01.

40
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.950
First, Set-DnsClientServerAddress points
the Ethernet adapter at 192.

41
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.400
168.

42
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:17.850
50.

43
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.300
10.

44
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:24.350
Test before you join: Resolve-DnsName with
type SRV for the DC record.

45
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.800
If DC01 and port 389 come back, the join
will find the DC.

46
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.050
Now Add-Computer, dash DomainName sa1.

47
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.500
lab.

48
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.750
Dash OUPath puts the computer object
straight into OU Servers.

49
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Dash Credential asks for the password in a
box, and dash Restart restarts SRV01 for

50
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.450
you.

51
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.500
After the restart, sign in as SA1
backslash Administrator and check the

52
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:51.950
domain.

53
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.000
Every joined computer gets a computer
object in Active Directory, with an

54
00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.650
account name like SRV01 dollar sign.

55
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.700
Without a choice, it lands in Computers.

56
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:09.350
That is a container, not an OU, and you
cannot link a Group Policy to it.

57
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.600
One fix is to pre-stage.

58
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.850
Before the join, right-click OU
Workstations, choose New, Computer, and

59
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.300
type CLIENT01.

60
00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:23.150
The dialog also says who may join this
computer; the default is Domain Admins.

61
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.600
When CLIENT01 joins later, it reuses this
object, already in the right OU.

62
00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:32.850
In PowerShell, New-ADComputer with dash
Path pre-stages CLIENT01 in Workstations.

63
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:37.900
Get-ADComputer with a filter of star lists
every computer object and its

64
00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:43.750
distinguished name, so you can see that
DC01, SRV01 and CLIENT01 are where they

65
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.200
belong, and nothing sits in CN equals
Computers.

66
00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:53.450
If one does, pipe it to Move-ADObject with
a target path, or in the console

67
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.700
right-click it and choose Move.

68
00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:01.950
As a concept only, redircmp changes where
every new join lands by default, for the

69
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.400
whole domain.

70
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:04.850
Now delegation.

71
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.300
The help desk needs to reset student
passwords.

72
00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.150
The lazy answer is Domain Admins for Ben
Ortiz.

73
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.400
Then one stolen help desk password owns
the whole domain.

74
00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:22.250
Least privilege means only the rights the
job needs, only where they are needed.

75
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.300
So bortiz is a member of GG-HelpDesk.

76
00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:30.750
That group gets one permission on OU
Students, and the user objects inside,

77
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.000
dpatel and emoss, inherit it.

78
00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:35.250
Always delegate to a group.

79
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.900
When staff change, you change the group
membership, not the permissions.

80
00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.150
The Delegation of Control Wizard sets this
up for you.

81
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.800
In Active Directory Users and Computers,
right-click OU Students and choose

82
00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.250
Delegate Control.

83
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:55.700
On the Users or Groups page, select Add,
type GG-HelpDesk and Check Names.

84
00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:01.150
On the Tasks to Delegate page, keep
Delegate the following common tasks, and

85
00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:06.600
select just one: Reset user passwords and
force password change at next logon.

86
00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:08.050
Nothing else.

87
00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:14.300
A custom task lets you pick object types
and rights yourself, but the common task

88
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.550
is exactly what we need.

89
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.000
Then Finish.

90
00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.650
What did the wizard actually do?

91
00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.700
It wrote permission entries on the OU.

92
00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:27.150
To see them, turn on View, Advanced
Features.

93
00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.800
Then open the properties of Students, the
Security tab, and Advanced.

94
00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.450
You find two entries for GG-HelpDesk.

95
00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.300
One allows Reset password.

96
00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:42.150
The other, shown as Special, lets the
group read and write pwdLastSet, which is

97
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.600
how force password change at next logon
works.

98
00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:52.250
Both apply to Descendant User objects: the
users inside the OU, not the OU itself or

99
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:53.700
its groups.

100
00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:58.750
The command-line tool dsacls lists every
permission entry on an AD object.

101
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.400
Point it at OU Students and filter with
findstr for helpdesk.

102
00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:08.050
Two lines come back: special access for
pwdLastSet, and Reset Password.

103
00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:13.100
In the full list, they sit in the section
inherited to user.

104
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:14.950
dsacls can also grant.

105
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.800
The slide shows the same two rights in
dsacls syntax: CA, control access, for

106
00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:25.450
Reset Password, and RP WP, read and write
property, for pwdLastSet.

107
00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:30.500
In the lab you use the wizard and only
read with dsacls.

108
00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:34.750
A service account is the identity a
service runs as.

109
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:39.000
LocalSystem is the most powerful, too much
for most apps.

110
00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:44.050
NetworkService and LocalService have low
local rights; on the network one appears

111
00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.100
as the computer, the other as anonymous.

112
00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:52.550
A virtual account, NT SERVICE backslash
name, gives one service its own identity

113
00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.000
on one server.

114
00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:58.250
A domain user works, but a person manages
its password.

115
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:03.300
Managed service accounts fix that:
standalone for one server, group for one

116
00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:07.150
or more servers, and delegated, new in
Server 2025.

117
00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:11.000
Why not just make a domain user called
svc-web?

118
00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:16.050
Because its password is typed by hand,
never expires, and never changes.

119
00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:21.100
Attackers can request its Kerberos tickets
and crack a weak password offline.

120
00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:22.950
A gMSA is different.

121
00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.800
Active Directory computes a long random
password and changes it every 30 days by

122
00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:30.250
default.

123
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:36.900
You need a KDS root key, created once per
forest, then the gMSA object, which names

124
00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.350
who may retrieve the password: only SRV01
dollar.

125
00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:46.600
SRV01 installs it, and a service logs on
as gmsa-web dollar with a blank password

126
00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:48.050
box.

127
00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.900
On DC01, Get-KdsRootKey shows nothing in a
new forest.

128
00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:53.750
Add-KdsRootKey creates the key.

129
00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:60.000
Normally DCs wait up to 10 hours before
using it, so the key can replicate.

130
00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.850
In our one-DC lab we use a documented
shortcut: dash EffectiveTime set to 10

131
00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:07.300
hours ago.

132
00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.550
Do not do that in production; there you
simply wait.

133
00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:15.000
Then New-ADServiceAccount gmsa-web, with
dash DNSHostName and dash

134
00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:18.050
PrincipalsAllowedToRetrieveManagedPassword
set to SRV01 dollar, in quotes.

135
00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.700
Get-ADServiceAccount confirms that SRV01
in OU Servers is the allowed principal.

136
00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:26.950
Now switch to SRV01, signed in as SA1
backslash Administrator.

137
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.600
SRV01 needs the Active Directory module,
so install the feature RSAT-AD-PowerShell.

138
00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:37.450
If it asks for source files, add dash
Source D colon backslash sources backslash

139
00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.100
sxs, the ISO in drive D.

140
00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:43.950
Then Install-ADServiceAccount gmsa-web,
which prints nothing when it works.

141
00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:46.200
Test-ADServiceAccount gmsa-web should say
True.

142
00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:50.450
True means SRV01 can get the password from
a DC.

143
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:56.700
Services log on as SA1 backslash gmsa-web
dollar, with a dollar sign like a computer

144
00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:58.150
account.

145
00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:03.600
Windows Server 2025 adds one more type,
the delegated managed service account, or

146
00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.050
dMSA.

147
00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.900
Its job is migration.

148
00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:13.150
You create a dMSA for an old domain user
service account, start the migration, then

149
00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:14.600
complete it.

150
00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:20.450
After that, the old password is blocked
and the dMSA takes its place, without

151
00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:21.900
reinstalling the service.

152
00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:27.750
The difference from a gMSA: the secret
stays on the domain controllers and is

153
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.400
tied to the machines you allow.

154
00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.850
You cannot migrate a gMSA to a dMSA.

155
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.900
In SA1 this is an overview only.

156
00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.550
Here are four mistakes to avoid.

157
00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:44.600
One: joining while DNS points at the old
server or at nothing.

158
00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:46.050
Set 192.

159
00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:47.500
168.

160
00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:48.950
50.

161
00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.000
10 first and test the SRV record.

162
00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:55.050
Two: leaving computers in CN equals
Computers.

163
00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.700
Use OUPath, pre-stage, or move them.

164
00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:03.150
Three: putting help desk staff in Domain
Admins, or delegating to one person.

165
00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:07.000
Delegate one task to a group on one OU.

166
00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:08.850
Four: Test-ADServiceAccount says False.

167
00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:14.300
Check the KDS root key, the allowed
principal SRV01 dollar, and that SRV01

168
00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.150
restarted after the join.

169
00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.600
Time to check yourself on the whole topic.

170
00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:21.450
First, CLIENT01 uses 192.

171
00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:22.900
168.

172
00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.350
50.

173
00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:25.800
20 for DNS.

174
00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.050
Why does the join fail, and what is the
fix?

175
00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:35.100
Second, bortiz can reset the password of
dpatel but not of alee.

176
00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:36.550
Why?

177
00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:42.800
Third, why use a gMSA for a web service
instead of a domain user account?

178
00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:49.450
And fourth, why did the lab date the KDS
root key 10 hours in the past?

179
00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:54.500
Pause the video, answer all four in your
own words, then continue.

180
00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:56.350
Here are the answers.

181
00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:57.800
One: 192.

182
00:10:58.050 --> 00:10:59.250
168.

183
00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.700
50.

184
00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.550
20 has no sa1.

185
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.600
lab zone, so CLIENT01 cannot find the SRV
record of a DC.

186
00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:09.450
Set DNS to 192.

187
00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:10.900
168.

188
00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:12.350
50.

189
00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:13.800
10, then join.

190
00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:17.250
Two: the right is on OU Students only.

191
00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:23.500
dpatel is in Students and inherits it;
alee is in Staff, where GG-HelpDesk has no

192
00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:24.950
rights.

193
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.400
Three: nobody knows or types the gMSA
password.

194
00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:33.850
AD changes it every 30 days, and only
SRV01 dollar may retrieve it.

195
00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:38.500
Four: so the key works at once in a one-DC
lab.

196
00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:40.750
In production, wait for replication.

197
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.400
Let's do it in the pod.

198
00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:48.450
On SRV01 I point DNS at DC01 and test the
SRV record.

199
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:54.700
DC01 answers on port 389, so I run
Add-Computer with the OU path for Servers.

200
00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:58.950
I type the password in the box, and SRV01
restarts.

201
00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:02.400
On DC01, Get-ADComputer shows SRV01 in OU
Servers.

202
00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:07.450
Next, I right-click Students, delegate the
password reset task to GG-HelpDesk, and

203
00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:12.500
then find the two entries in Advanced
Security Settings and with dsacls.

204
00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:17.550
Finally I create the KDS root key and
gmsa-web, and on SRV01

205
00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.000
Test-ADServiceAccount returns True.

206
00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:20.450
To wrap up.

207
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.300
Point DNS at 192.

208
00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:23.750
168.

209
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.200
50.

210
00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:29.850
10 first, then join, with System
Properties, Settings, or Add-Computer with

211
00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:31.300
an OU path.

212
00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:35.950
Computer objects belong in Servers or
Workstations, not in the Computers

213
00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:37.400
container.

214
00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:43.650
Delegate one task to a group on one OU,
and read the result with dsacls.

215
00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:48.300
For services, prefer virtual accounts and
gMSAs over domain user accounts.

216
00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:49.750
In Lab 4.

217
00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:56.000
3, about 75 minutes and practice only, you
rebuild the OU tree, join two computers,

218
00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:58.250
delegate resets and test gmsa-web.
